    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's start by expressing 11! and 12!.\n11! = 1 * 2 * 3 * ... * 11\n12! = 1 * 2 * 3 * ... * 12\n\nNow let's add these expressions together:\n11! + 12! = (1 * 2 * 3 * ... * 11) + (1 * 2 * 3 * ... * 12)\n\nSince we are adding two consecutive factorials, there will be a common factor of (1 * 2 * 3 * ... * 11) in the expansion of 12! - 11!. This factor will cancel out with the first 11! in the sum.\nSo, we can simplify the equation to:\n12! + 11! + 12! - 11! = (12! + 11!) - (11! + 12!) = 2 * 12! + 2 * 11! - 2 * 11! - 12! = 2 * 12! - 12!\n\nUsing Simon's Favorite Factoring Trick, we can factor out a common factor of 2 from the left-hand side of the equation:\n2 * (12! - 1) = 2 * (12 * 11!)\n\nNow, we just need to find the prime factors of the right-hand side and identify the largest one.\n12 * 11 = 132\nSince 132 is not prime, we need to factor it further:\n132 = 2 * 66\n66 = 2 * 33\n33 = 3 * 11\n\nSo, 132 = 2^2 * 3 * 11\n\nThe largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! is 11.",
